# Cursor rules branch

`.cursor/` is published on `origin/cursor-rules` in the `pabdelhay/MuseScore` fork. In a normal checkout it stays untracked, so feature branches and pull requests to `upstream` do not include it.

`cursor-rules` is `upstream/main` plus the `.cursor/` commit. Publish `.cursor/` only on that branch. Leave `main`, `pablos-ideas`, and feature branches untouched by it.

## Fetch into a checkout

`.git/info/exclude` is local to the clone. Add the ignore lines once, before the first commit, then copy the rules into the working tree without staging them. Skip the `Add-Content` block when `.git/info/exclude` already lists `.cursor/`. Run this from the repo root:

```powershell
@"

# Local notes. Never commit these; a .gitignore edit would show up in a pull request.
.cursor/
IDEAS.md
aqtinstall.log
"@ | Add-Content .git\info\exclude

git fetch origin cursor-rules
git restore --source=origin/cursor-rules --worktree -- .cursor
```

`git restore --worktree` writes the files and leaves the index alone. The exclude lines then keep `git add` from picking `.cursor/` up. `git check-ignore -v .cursor` should name `.git/info/exclude`.

## Publish rule edits

Do this only when the user asks to save or publish Cursor rules. From the repo root, set aside the local `.cursor/`, commit it on `cursor-rules`, push that branch, then put the untracked copy back on the branch you were using:

```powershell
$branch = git branch --show-current
$saved = Join-Path $env:TEMP "musescore-cursor-save"
if (Test-Path $saved) { Remove-Item -Recurse -Force $saved }
Copy-Item -Recurse .cursor $saved
Remove-Item -Recurse -Force .cursor

git fetch origin
git fetch upstream
git switch cursor-rules
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { git switch -c cursor-rules --track origin/cursor-rules }
if ((git branch --show-current) -ne "cursor-rules") {
  Copy-Item -Recurse $saved .cursor
  throw "Stayed on $branch. Restored .cursor and skipped the commit."
}

git merge upstream/main -m "Merge upstream/main into cursor-rules."
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw "Merge failed. Stay on cursor-rules and resolve it before committing." }

Remove-Item -Recurse -Force .cursor
Copy-Item -Recurse $saved .cursor
git add -f .cursor
$outside = git diff --cached --name-only | Where-Object { $_ -notlike ".cursor/*" }
if ($outside) {
  git reset
  throw "Refusing to commit paths outside .cursor/."
}
if (git diff --cached --quiet) {
  Write-Host "No .cursor changes to commit."
} else {
  git commit -m "Update Cursor rules."
}
git push origin cursor-rules

git switch $branch
git restore --source=cursor-rules --worktree -- .cursor
```

`git add -f` is required because exclude ignores `.cursor/`. The branch check and the path check run before any commit. `git switch` back removes the tracked copy; the restore puts it back untracked. Afterward, `git status` on the daily branch must not list `.cursor/`.
